1
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:07,760
Welcome to Anticipa, a prevention autopilot for a
whole population.

2
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:13,323
It reads health records and clinical notes, applies
approved guidelines, plans outreach around

3
00:00:13,353 --> 00:00:17,130
real clinic capacity, and follows every case until
the care is done.

4
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:20,062
Every resident in this walkthrough is synthetic,

5
00:00:20,092 --> 00:00:22,310
so no real patient data appears anywhere.

6
00:00:23,196 --> 00:00:27,226
Three promises sit on this screen, and they shape
everything that follows.

7
00:00:27,556 --> 00:00:30,566
Every fact is traced to the exact quote it came
from.

8
00:00:30,896 --> 00:00:35,796
Eligibility is decided by approved, versioned rules,
never by AI.

9
00:00:36,126 --> 00:00:40,046
And every action is audited, with each role limited
to what it needs.

10
00:00:40,931 --> 00:00:42,031
There are two ways in.

11
00:00:42,361 --> 00:00:47,271
Anyone exploring the product can continue with
Google, which gives read only access.

12
00:00:47,601 --> 00:00:50,156
Staff sign in with an email and a password,

13
00:00:50,186 --> 00:00:52,981
because an administrator creates every account.

14
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:58,129
Anticipa has five roles: population administrator,
care coordinator,

15
00:00:58,159 --> 00:01:02,220
clinical reviewer, system administrator and a read
only observer.

16
00:01:02,550 --> 00:01:06,132
Each role sees only the pages and actions its job
needs,

17
00:01:06,162 --> 00:01:09,100
and the server enforces that on every request.

18
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:13,130
I will sign in as the system administrator, who can
see every page.

19
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:16,614
Everything that follows is a live screen running
against

20
00:01:16,644 --> 00:01:18,490
the real database, not a mock up.

21
00:01:22,753 --> 00:01:26,273
This is the Command Center, the first screen a
coordinator sees.

22
00:01:26,603 --> 00:01:30,253
Four headline figures run across the top: total
population,

23
00:01:30,283 --> 00:01:34,183
residents with care gaps, open care gaps, and the
closure rate.

24
00:01:34,513 --> 00:01:36,808
Every card reads live from the database,

25
00:01:36,838 --> 00:01:39,773
and the badge shows when the data was last updated.

26
00:01:40,685 --> 00:01:44,829
On the left, programs at a glance shows the share of
each program's

27
00:01:44,859 --> 00:01:47,135
care gaps that are already completed.

28
00:01:47,465 --> 00:01:51,509
Below it, open cases are split by priority, so a
coordinator

29
00:01:51,539 --> 00:01:54,225
sees at once where the urgent work sits.

30
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:58,539
The centre of the page is a tilted glass map of
India,

31
00:01:58,569 --> 00:02:01,540
shaded by care gap cases per hundred residents.

32
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:04,600
Select a state and the whole page narrows to it.

33
00:02:04,930 --> 00:02:10,224
Here is Madhya Pradesh: population, due for care,
open gaps and closure rate all

34
00:02:10,254 --> 00:02:13,950
change, and so do the programs and priorities beside
it.

35
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:19,184
Select a district such as Bhopal and every card
narrows again, while the numbered

36
00:02:19,214 --> 00:02:22,900
panels keep the India, state and district views side
by side.

37
00:02:23,230 --> 00:02:25,520
Back to India restores the full picture.

38
00:02:27,717 --> 00:02:33,011
The care journey funnel follows residents from the
whole population to closed care gaps:

39
00:02:33,041 --> 00:02:37,247
evaluated, due for care, case opened, planned, in
outreach and closed.

40
00:02:37,577 --> 00:02:41,295
Under each chart, View as table gives the same
numbers as text,

41
00:02:41,325 --> 00:02:43,377
for anyone who prefers or needs it.

42
00:02:44,267 --> 00:02:47,311
Key takeaways turn the figures into plain sentences,

43
00:02:47,341 --> 00:02:50,797
such as which district has the most care gaps per
resident.

44
00:02:51,127 --> 00:02:53,987
The closure trend plots cases closed each day.

45
00:02:54,317 --> 00:02:59,479
And equity across districts shows how much of each
district's care gap load has reached

46
00:02:59,509 --> 00:03:03,357
a plan, with the gap between the best and worst
covered district.

47
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:08,490
Active alerts apply threshold rules, and each one
has a definition you can open.

48
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:12,721
Failed background jobs, clinic capacity, outreach
backlog and evidence

49
00:03:12,751 --> 00:03:14,800
awaiting review are all watched here.

50
00:03:15,130 --> 00:03:18,023
The operations snapshot adds pipeline progress,

51
00:03:18,053 --> 00:03:21,320
documents registered, AI spend and cases in
outreach.

52
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:26,160
Recent activity lists the latest audited actions,
with a link to the full audit log.

53
00:03:27,070 --> 00:03:30,450
Two selectors in the top bar apply on every page.

54
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:34,165
The district scope narrows any view to a single
district,

55
00:03:34,195 --> 00:03:37,700
and the reporting window can run from seven to
ninety days.

56
00:03:38,030 --> 00:03:41,540
Changing either one refreshes the cards you are
looking at.

57
00:03:50,244 --> 00:03:52,634
Population is the programme manager's view.

58
00:03:52,964 --> 00:03:57,324
The same four headline figures sit on top, and
population at a glance lists

59
00:03:57,354 --> 00:04:00,718
exactly what is loaded: residents, residents with
complete

60
00:04:00,748 --> 00:04:03,644
records, families, clinical records and documents.

61
00:04:03,974 --> 00:04:07,264
The dataset covers four states, ten districts and
thirty clinics.

62
00:04:07,594 --> 00:04:11,334
Each figure has an information icon that explains
how it is defined.

63
00:04:12,217 --> 00:04:15,417
The same map drills from India to state to district.

64
00:04:15,747 --> 00:04:20,049
Planning coverage shows what share of care gaps has
already reached a

65
00:04:20,079 --> 00:04:23,187
plan, and the donut splits open cases by priority.

66
00:04:24,095 --> 00:04:28,179
Key programmes lists, for every program, the share
of residents who are

67
00:04:28,209 --> 00:04:31,135
up to date, with the counts behind each percentage.

68
00:04:31,465 --> 00:04:35,375
Beside it, the population health trend plots cases
closed per day.

69
00:04:36,275 --> 00:04:39,855
The care journey flow, equity and capacity cards sit
together.

70
00:04:40,185 --> 00:04:45,809
Equity compares districts two ways, by residents who
reached a plan and by residents due for

71
00:04:45,839 --> 00:04:49,865
care, and reports the gap between the highest and
lowest district.

72
00:04:50,195 --> 00:04:53,247
Capacity shows clinic seats in the planning window,

73
00:04:53,277 --> 00:04:55,845
how many are reserved, and the utilization.

74
00:04:56,751 --> 00:05:01,431
Age and sex shows every age band for men and women,
and selecting a band marks it.

75
00:05:01,761 --> 00:05:04,898
Outreach and engagement carries the Simulated tag,

76
00:05:04,928 --> 00:05:07,811
because no message ever reaches a real person.

77
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:11,660
Loop closure then counts cases from identification
through

78
00:05:11,690 --> 00:05:13,741
plan, engage, attend and complete.

79
00:05:14,629 --> 00:05:19,732
The population profile finishes the page with age
band, sex, district and preferred

80
00:05:19,762 --> 00:05:23,009
language, which here means English, Hindi and
Arabic.

81
00:05:23,339 --> 00:05:26,989
Below it, every figure on the page is defined in
plain words.

82
00:05:31,224 --> 00:05:32,624
Insights is the explorer.

83
00:05:32,954 --> 00:05:37,951
Four tabs, Population, Needs and gaps, Trends and
Equity, give four views of the

84
00:05:37,981 --> 00:05:41,344
same data, and the three headline figures stay on
top.

85
00:05:41,674 --> 00:05:47,013
On the Population tab, the View by selector
recolours the district map by a different measure,

86
00:05:47,043 --> 00:05:50,954
and the age and sex chart switches between
demographics and priority.

87
00:05:55,780 --> 00:06:00,530
The population explorer slices the population by age
group, sex and area.

88
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:05,404
Choose fifty to fifty nine, female, and Bhopal, and
the donut and the figures

89
00:06:05,434 --> 00:06:08,790
beside it update: residents due for care, open care
gaps,

90
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:12,830
care gaps per hundred residents, closure rate and
planning coverage.

91
00:06:15,875 --> 00:06:19,896
Key population insights writes the findings out in
plain language:

92
00:06:19,926 --> 00:06:24,377
where care gap density is highest, how many high
priority cases are open,

93
00:06:24,407 --> 00:06:27,875
and where the largest share of residents is due for
care.

94
00:06:29,971 --> 00:06:34,697
Needs and gaps shows open care gaps by priority, and
then every district

95
00:06:34,727 --> 00:06:38,131
with its open cases and how many are already closed.

96
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:44,720
Trends plots cases closed per day and cases opened
per day across the reporting window,

97
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:48,330
and every chart has a table view with the same
numbers.

98
00:06:49,217 --> 00:06:53,237
Equity compares planning coverage and closure rate
for every district,

99
00:06:53,267 --> 00:06:57,867
and states the gap between the best and the worst
district in percentage points.

100
00:06:58,197 --> 00:07:02,937
A small gap means residents in every district have
similar access to care.

101
00:07:07,182 --> 00:07:09,282
Residents is the search tool for people.

102
00:07:09,612 --> 00:07:14,242
Search by identifier, or filter by district, sex,
language and age band.

103
00:07:14,572 --> 00:07:19,402
The list is paged, and every row shows age, sex,
district and a short identifier.

104
00:07:20,294 --> 00:07:23,841
Selecting a resident opens a summary beside the
list: age,

105
00:07:23,871 --> 00:07:28,466
language, district, family links and documents, with
tabs for the overview,

106
00:07:28,496 --> 00:07:32,104
the care journey, the family, and the records and
evidence.

107
00:07:32,434 --> 00:07:34,814
Open full record goes to the whole file.

108
00:07:39,085 --> 00:07:44,216
The full record opens with the resident summary and
the record counts by category,

109
00:07:44,246 --> 00:07:48,055
from encounters and conditions to medications and
procedures.

110
00:07:48,385 --> 00:07:51,233
Below sit the care gaps raised for this resident,

111
00:07:51,263 --> 00:07:54,465
and a timeline of every clinical event with its
source.

112
00:07:56,631 --> 00:08:00,974
The consent section lists contact preferences for
every channel: SMS,

113
00:08:01,004 --> 00:08:06,171
WhatsApp, phone call and email, plus whether
documents may be read by an AI model.

114
00:08:06,501 --> 00:08:10,169
No decision on file means permit, which is a
synthetic default,

115
00:08:10,199 --> 00:08:13,221
and opting out of one channel keeps the others open.

116
00:08:14,119 --> 00:08:15,229
Now the care journey.

117
00:08:15,559 --> 00:08:18,835
This page follows one resident through five stages:

118
00:08:18,865 --> 00:08:21,039
Read, Decide, Plan, Act and Close.

119
00:08:21,369 --> 00:08:24,210
Each stage is complete, in progress or not reached,

120
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:26,349
and each shows the evidence behind it.

121
00:08:27,245 --> 00:08:30,670
Read shows the facts the AI extracted from this
resident's

122
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:32,755
notes, nine facts from three notes.

123
00:08:33,085 --> 00:08:35,065
This resident's notes are in Arabic.

124
00:08:35,395 --> 00:08:39,099
Every fact carries its exact quote, with the
verification badge,

125
00:08:39,129 --> 00:08:43,125
and Show quote in note jumps to those words in the
original document.

126
00:08:44,018 --> 00:08:48,348
Decide lists the nine guidelines evaluated for this
resident, two of them due.

127
00:08:48,678 --> 00:08:52,789
Select Why for any guideline and the explainer
opens: the outcome,

128
00:08:52,819 --> 00:08:56,867
the deciding rule and its version, the reason code
and the engine

129
00:08:56,897 --> 00:08:59,878
version, then every criterion with pass or fail.

130
00:09:00,208 --> 00:09:01,668
A versioned rule decides.

131
00:09:01,998 --> 00:09:05,158
The AI reads the notes and never decides who is due.

132
00:09:06,044 --> 00:09:10,604
Plan shows the slot the capacity planner proposed,
at which simulated clinic,

133
00:09:10,634 --> 00:09:13,464
for which service, and why that slot was chosen.

134
00:09:13,794 --> 00:09:17,685
Act records the outreach: the message, its language,
its delivery,

135
00:09:17,715 --> 00:09:21,664
the resident's reply and the appointment, which here
was a no show.

136
00:09:22,567 --> 00:09:25,747
Close happens only with verified completion
evidence.

137
00:09:26,077 --> 00:09:30,707
This cervical case is still open, so the page says
so and repeats the outreach.

138
00:09:31,037 --> 00:09:36,431
Switch to this resident's other case, a BRCA risk
assessment, and every stage is complete:

139
00:09:36,461 --> 00:09:40,227
the status history, the verified proof, and the
closure reason.

140
00:09:41,119 --> 00:09:44,909
Family history draws the stored relatives as a
pedigree.

141
00:09:45,239 --> 00:09:49,353
Each relative shows how they are linked, with the
source of that link,

142
00:09:49,383 --> 00:09:53,439
and any cancer history on file with the clinical
record it came from.

143
00:09:53,769 --> 00:09:56,259
Here the spouse has a tongue cancer history.

144
00:09:56,589 --> 00:10:01,362
Facts about relatives that cannot be tied to exactly
one stored relative are

145
00:10:01,392 --> 00:10:04,839
shown for transparency and never added to the
pedigree.

146
00:10:09,114 --> 00:10:12,215
Evidence and guidelines is where unstructured
clinical

147
00:10:12,245 --> 00:10:13,954
notes become structured facts.

148
00:10:14,284 --> 00:10:18,424
Pick any resident with extracted evidence, or open
one by identifier.

149
00:10:18,754 --> 00:10:23,357
The clinical note panel shows the original text,
here a family history narrative written

150
00:10:23,387 --> 00:10:26,464
in Hindi, and the arrows move through the resident's
notes.

151
00:10:27,356 --> 00:10:31,316
The extracted evidence panel lists every fact found
in this note.

152
00:10:31,646 --> 00:10:34,684
Each one has a concept, the relative it is about,

153
00:10:34,714 --> 00:10:38,316
whether it is stated or negated, and a verification
badge.

154
00:10:38,646 --> 00:10:42,974
This note says there is no history of ovarian cancer
in the mother,

155
00:10:43,004 --> 00:10:46,226
so the fact is recorded as negated, not as a risk.

156
00:10:47,125 --> 00:10:50,565
Select a fact and the page finds its exact quote in
the note.

157
00:10:50,895 --> 00:10:54,862
Quote verified, exact, means deterministic text
matching found those

158
00:10:54,892 --> 00:10:57,155
same characters in the source document.

159
00:10:57,485 --> 00:11:00,705
A fact whose quote cannot be verified is never
accepted.

160
00:11:01,609 --> 00:11:05,202
Guideline matching then applies every approved rule
to this

161
00:11:05,232 --> 00:11:07,289
resident, one criterion at a time.

162
00:11:07,619 --> 00:11:11,160
Each criterion passes or fails, and the care
decision on the

163
00:11:11,190 --> 00:11:13,599
right states the outcome with its reason.

164
00:11:13,929 --> 00:11:17,787
Here the resident is not eligible, because the
diabetes programme

165
00:11:17,817 --> 00:11:19,999
screens adults aged thirty and above.

166
00:11:20,895 --> 00:11:24,459
The strip along the bottom summarises the pipeline
for this resident:

167
00:11:24,489 --> 00:11:27,064
four unstructured notes, fourteen traceable facts,

168
00:11:27,094 --> 00:11:30,295
nine deterministic evaluations, and the resulting
care action.

169
00:11:31,191 --> 00:11:35,311
The review queue gathers facts from across the
population that need a human.

170
00:11:35,641 --> 00:11:39,341
Only facts whose quote is verified against the note
can be accepted.

171
00:11:39,671 --> 00:11:43,445
Choose needs review, accepted or rejected, and each
row shows

172
00:11:43,475 --> 00:11:46,001
the concept, the relative and the status.

173
00:11:46,892 --> 00:11:50,275
Selecting a fact opens its detail: the extracted
value,

174
00:11:50,305 --> 00:11:54,743
why it needs review, here low extraction confidence,
the verified quote,

175
00:11:54,773 --> 00:11:58,032
and which model extracted it with the prompt
version.

176
00:11:58,362 --> 00:12:00,517
Show in document jumps back to the note,

177
00:12:00,547 --> 00:12:03,412
and Open journey goes to the resident's full
journey.

178
00:12:07,659 --> 00:12:10,971
Guidelines lists every guideline with its topic, its
authoritative

179
00:12:11,001 --> 00:12:13,249
source, and its latest and approved versions.

180
00:12:13,579 --> 00:12:17,546
The United States Preventive Services Task Force
provides the cancer screening

181
00:12:17,576 --> 00:12:20,468
guidelines, and the Ministry of Health and Family
Welfare

182
00:12:20,498 --> 00:12:22,979
provides the India national programme guidelines.

183
00:12:23,309 --> 00:12:26,699
Eligibility is decided by approved, versioned rules
in code.

184
00:12:27,029 --> 00:12:30,169
AI reads documents and never decides who is
eligible.

185
00:12:31,068 --> 00:12:33,881
A version moves from draft to in review to approved,

186
00:12:33,911 --> 00:12:36,068
and the version state filter shows each.

187
00:12:36,398 --> 00:12:39,304
The India programme guidelines for hypertension,

188
00:12:39,334 --> 00:12:41,628
diabetes and oral cancer are approved.

189
00:12:41,958 --> 00:12:45,328
Breast examination and cervical visual inspection
are drafts

190
00:12:45,358 --> 00:12:47,198
that have not yet been submitted.

191
00:12:48,084 --> 00:12:52,494
Guideline Studio is where a clinical reviewer works
on rule versions.

192
00:12:52,824 --> 00:12:55,844
Choose a guideline and a version, and its rules
appear.

193
00:12:56,174 --> 00:12:59,237
Here is the clinical breast examination guideline,

194
00:12:59,267 --> 00:13:03,134
a draft that has not been submitted, so it can still
be edited.

195
00:13:04,034 --> 00:13:09,444
Each rule is tagged as a prerequisite, an inclusion,
an exclusion or a timing rule.

196
00:13:09,774 --> 00:13:13,999
It shows what happens when its criteria are met,
when they are not met,

197
00:13:14,029 --> 00:13:17,834
and when the evidence is missing, together with its
reason code.

198
00:13:18,164 --> 00:13:21,554
Numbers such as the minimum age are editable fields.

199
00:13:22,452 --> 00:13:27,083
Before anything is submitted, a dry run evaluates
the draft on real records

200
00:13:27,113 --> 00:13:29,692
and compares it with the approved version.

201
00:13:30,022 --> 00:13:34,557
Choose a sample, either the ten thousand resident AI
cohort or a random

202
00:13:34,587 --> 00:13:37,707
sample of one thousand to ten thousand residents,

203
00:13:37,737 --> 00:13:40,792
narrow it to a district if you like, and run it.

204
00:13:41,122 --> 00:13:42,272
Nothing is saved.

205
00:13:45,284 --> 00:13:50,134
The headline says how many residents in the sample
are due under this version,

206
00:13:50,164 --> 00:13:55,704
and estimates the number across the whole population
with a ninety five percent interval.

207
00:13:56,034 --> 00:14:00,884
A small sample answers in seconds, and the page
shows how long this run took.

208
00:14:01,767 --> 00:14:05,285
Outcomes under this version counts eligible, not
eligible,

209
00:14:05,315 --> 00:14:09,567
already completed, insufficient evidence and
requires clinical review.

210
00:14:09,897 --> 00:14:13,819
Due by district and due by age band show where the
due residents are,

211
00:14:13,849 --> 00:14:16,797
and top reasons explains why the others are not due.

212
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:20,803
Example residents lists real cases from the sample,

213
00:14:20,833 --> 00:14:23,710
each with its outcome and the reason behind it.

214
00:14:24,040 --> 00:14:26,660
The notes at the foot of the page are the guarantee.

215
00:14:26,990 --> 00:14:32,647
Nothing was saved, no care gap case or outreach was
created, the same deterministic

216
00:14:32,677 --> 00:14:36,280
engine as production ran, and no AI model was
called.

217
00:14:37,164 --> 00:14:40,434
Send for review hands the version to a clinical
reviewer.

218
00:14:40,764 --> 00:14:44,167
Approval stays with the reviewer, and nothing
changes for

219
00:14:44,197 --> 00:14:46,094
any resident until they approve.

220
00:14:50,382 --> 00:14:51,862
Care gaps is the case list.

221
00:14:52,192 --> 00:14:55,802
Every case names the guideline and the rule version
that raised it.

222
00:14:56,132 --> 00:15:01,647
Four tiles summarise the work: residents with care
gaps, high priority cases still open,

223
00:15:01,677 --> 00:15:05,302
cases closed with verified evidence, and the closure
rate.

224
00:15:06,189 --> 00:15:08,219
Filter by status, priority and district.

225
00:15:08,549 --> 00:15:11,722
Statuses follow the life of a case: identified,
pending review,

226
00:15:11,752 --> 00:15:15,180
approved for planning, awaiting capacity, planned,
outreach pending,

227
00:15:15,210 --> 00:15:18,841
outreach completed, appointment scheduled, awaiting
completion evidence,

228
00:15:18,871 --> 00:15:21,739
escalated, completed, closed not completed and
cancelled.

229
00:15:22,636 --> 00:15:25,496
The table lists each case with its care gap,

230
00:15:25,526 --> 00:15:28,386
priority, status, rule outcome and district.

231
00:15:28,716 --> 00:15:30,866
Open a case to see everything about it.

232
00:15:31,762 --> 00:15:36,362
The case page starts with the case itself and the
eligibility decision.

233
00:15:36,692 --> 00:15:40,663
The decision is made by the versioned rule, not by
an AI model,

234
00:15:40,693 --> 00:15:44,982
and the page names the guideline, the rule and its
source reference.

235
00:15:45,879 --> 00:15:47,699
Status history records every transition.

236
00:15:48,029 --> 00:15:52,443
The case was created as identified, approved
automatically under an approved rule version,

237
00:15:52,473 --> 00:15:55,208
planned when an allocation was confirmed, then
outreach,

238
00:15:55,238 --> 00:15:59,059
appointment, awaiting completion evidence, and
completed on verified evidence.

239
00:15:59,946 --> 00:16:05,196
Change status offers only valid lifecycle
transitions, and it asks for a reason.

240
00:16:05,526 --> 00:16:09,126
Every change is recorded in the history and in the
audit log.

241
00:16:10,025 --> 00:16:14,106
Further down, Outreach shows the simulated message
the resident would receive,

242
00:16:14,136 --> 00:16:17,531
Appointments shows the simulated booking, and
Completion evidence

243
00:16:17,561 --> 00:16:20,535
shows the verified proof, followed by the closure
reason.

244
00:16:21,430 --> 00:16:24,976
The conversation section holds two way messages with
the

245
00:16:25,006 --> 00:16:27,210
resident, entered by a coordinator.

246
00:16:27,540 --> 00:16:29,590
Appointment questions get an answer.

247
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:32,230
Every health question goes to a clinician.

248
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:36,287
Here is a case where the resident asked whether a
family history

249
00:16:36,317 --> 00:16:38,810
of breast cancer means she will get it too.

250
00:16:39,694 --> 00:16:41,914
The safety layer did not try to answer.

251
00:16:42,244 --> 00:16:47,253
It recognised a medical question, escalated the
case, and replied with approved

252
00:16:47,283 --> 00:16:50,124
text that passes the question to a clinician.

253
00:16:50,454 --> 00:16:53,858
The case now reads escalated, waiting for a
clinician,

254
00:16:53,888 --> 00:16:56,784
and the answer is never improvised by a model.

255
00:16:57,681 --> 00:16:59,621
Residents write in their own language.

256
00:16:59,951 --> 00:17:04,361
Choose Hindi, send an appointment question, and the
reply comes back in Hindi.

257
00:17:04,691 --> 00:17:08,201
The message is simulated, and nothing reaches a real
person.

258
00:17:13,546 --> 00:17:17,226
Arabic works the same way, and the thread switches
to right to left.

259
00:17:17,556 --> 00:17:22,846
Medical emergencies and questions are always handed
to a clinician, in any language.

260
00:17:29,733 --> 00:17:34,659
In the evaluation of seventy one test conversations
in English, Hindi and Arabic,

261
00:17:34,689 --> 00:17:38,086
all forty cases that required escalation were
escalated,

262
00:17:38,116 --> 00:17:42,553
and none of the nineteen replies written by a model
gave clinical advice.

263
00:17:46,796 --> 00:17:49,286
Programs groups the work by screening program.

264
00:17:49,616 --> 00:17:54,589
The active program card names the guideline behind
it, with the eligible population,

265
00:17:54,619 --> 00:17:57,686
the active care gaps and the care already completed.

266
00:17:58,016 --> 00:18:00,126
Each figure opens the matching cases.

267
00:18:03,531 --> 00:18:06,231
Next best actions turn those numbers into work.

268
00:18:06,561 --> 00:18:11,889
Review the high priority cases, book appointments
for residents who were contacted and have none

269
00:18:11,919 --> 00:18:15,851
yet, and review the completion evidence that is
waiting for a decision.

270
00:18:16,181 --> 00:18:18,341
Each action opens the cases it counts.

271
00:18:19,241 --> 00:18:23,835
All preventive care programs lists everything that
runs: cervical, oral cancer,

272
00:18:23,865 --> 00:18:29,455
breast, colorectal, diabetes and hypertension
screening, the two BRCA steps for hereditary risk,

273
00:18:29,485 --> 00:18:33,261
and colorectal screening for people with a first
degree relative.

274
00:18:33,591 --> 00:18:36,231
Selecting a program makes it the active one at the
top.

275
00:18:40,961 --> 00:18:42,851
Care planning decides who gets a slot.

276
00:18:43,181 --> 00:18:46,212
Four steps run along the top: select the population,

277
00:18:46,242 --> 00:18:49,981
define the interventions, check capacity, and
generate the plan.

278
00:18:50,311 --> 00:18:53,228
Allocations are proposals until they are confirmed,

279
00:18:53,258 --> 00:18:55,251
and confirming one reserves a seat.

280
00:18:56,137 --> 00:19:00,992
The plan summary shows what the planner worked with:
the cases waiting for a slot,

281
00:19:01,022 --> 00:19:05,937
nine screenings that each have an approved
guideline, and the seats open this week.

282
00:19:06,267 --> 00:19:10,410
The latest run placed about one hundred and four
thousand of one

283
00:19:10,440 --> 00:19:12,887
hundred and sixty five thousand cases.

284
00:19:13,788 --> 00:19:17,817
What changed compares the latest run with the one
before it,

285
00:19:17,847 --> 00:19:22,823
so a coordinator can see exactly why coverage moved:
the cases in the run,

286
00:19:22,853 --> 00:19:26,138
how many were allocated a slot, and the coverage.

287
00:19:27,042 --> 00:19:30,972
Why cases were left out gives a reason for every
case without a slot.

288
00:19:31,302 --> 00:19:33,972
Capacity exhausted accounts for most of them.

289
00:19:34,302 --> 00:19:38,268
The rest are residents who denied consent, cases
outside travel

290
00:19:38,298 --> 00:19:40,932
eligibility, and a resident time conflict.

291
00:19:41,823 --> 00:19:43,843
Three scenarios change the planner's settings.

292
00:19:44,173 --> 00:19:47,083
Population wide maximises preventive coverage.

293
00:19:47,413 --> 00:19:51,373
High risk first weights urgent and high priority
cases far above the rest.

294
00:19:51,703 --> 00:19:54,793
Equity focus adds a weight across districts and
languages.

295
00:19:55,123 --> 00:19:56,923
Every run keeps its settings for review.

296
00:19:57,820 --> 00:20:02,500
The Capacity tab shows total seats, reserved seats,
what remains and the seat use.

297
00:20:02,830 --> 00:20:07,268
The chart sets planned allocation against available
capacity in every district,

298
00:20:07,298 --> 00:20:11,340
and the table lists each district's total, reserved
and remaining seats.

299
00:20:12,231 --> 00:20:13,941
Allocate lists the planning runs.

300
00:20:14,271 --> 00:20:18,717
Opening a run shows its allocations, the cases that
were not allocated

301
00:20:18,747 --> 00:20:22,553
and why, and the equity of the run: coverage by
district and

302
00:20:22,583 --> 00:20:25,621
by language, so no group is quietly left behind.

303
00:20:26,506 --> 00:20:30,266
Track keeps the history of runs, each with its
planning period,

304
00:20:30,296 --> 00:20:34,296
the cases considered, the number allocated and when
it was created.

305
00:20:49,419 --> 00:20:52,019
Operations is where capacity meets reality.

306
00:20:52,349 --> 00:20:56,769
Four tabs cover scheduling, the resource view,
rebalancing and the site network.

307
00:20:57,099 --> 00:20:59,982
The care network shows thirty clinics in ten
districts,

308
00:21:00,012 --> 00:21:02,789
all open, with each district's share of seats
booked.

309
00:21:03,693 --> 00:21:06,633
Dots are clinics, and labels show seats booked.

310
00:21:06,963 --> 00:21:09,763
Select a district and the page filters to it.

311
00:21:12,169 --> 00:21:15,977
The appointment schedule shows seats booked out of
seats offered,

312
00:21:16,007 --> 00:21:17,689
for every slot at one clinic.

313
00:21:18,019 --> 00:21:20,009
Each cell is a service at a time.

314
00:21:20,339 --> 00:21:23,779
Pick a clinic, step through the days, or switch to
the week view.

315
00:21:24,109 --> 00:21:30,019
Times are UTC, and slots are seats the clinic
offers, not individual appointments.

316
00:21:30,904 --> 00:21:33,244
Scheduling also summarises the network.

317
00:21:33,574 --> 00:21:38,355
It counts the simulated bookings that reached
completed, the capacity utilization with

318
00:21:38,385 --> 00:21:41,824
seats booked out of seats offered, and the available
capacity.

319
00:21:42,154 --> 00:21:45,806
The median time to attend says plainly that it is
not available,

320
00:21:45,836 --> 00:21:47,474
because no source records it.

321
00:21:48,364 --> 00:21:52,072
Capacity by district gives every district's share of
seats

322
00:21:52,102 --> 00:21:54,134
booked and the seats still open.

323
00:21:54,464 --> 00:21:57,310
These are the same seats the planner allocates,

324
00:21:57,340 --> 00:22:00,124
so what you see here is what planning can use.

325
00:22:01,009 --> 00:22:05,242
Rebalancing shows where seats are overloaded and
where they are free:

326
00:22:05,272 --> 00:22:10,309
the districts above ninety five percent use, and the
districts below seventy five.

327
00:22:10,639 --> 00:22:15,025
When nothing is overloaded it says so, and it points
to the planning studio,

328
00:22:15,055 --> 00:22:19,209
because moving bookings is done by a planning run,
not from this screen.

329
00:22:20,118 --> 00:22:22,897
The resource view lists every clinic with its
district,

330
00:22:22,927 --> 00:22:24,838
the services it offers and its status.

331
00:22:25,168 --> 00:22:28,227
Each simulated clinic offers a mix of blood
pressure,

332
00:22:28,257 --> 00:22:31,958
glucose, cervical, breast, colorectal and oral
cancer screening.

333
00:22:32,288 --> 00:22:35,231
Operational alerts list anything that needs
attention,

334
00:22:35,261 --> 00:22:36,938
such as failed background jobs.

335
00:22:41,208 --> 00:22:44,698
Outreach follows every case from the first message
to completed care.

336
00:22:45,028 --> 00:22:48,350
The funnel counts cases at each step: contacted,
delivered,

337
00:22:48,380 --> 00:22:51,758
accepted, booked, attended, evidence verified and
completed.

338
00:22:52,088 --> 00:22:55,966
A banner states plainly that every message, response
and appointment is

339
00:22:55,996 --> 00:22:58,608
simulated, and no message reaches a real person.

340
00:22:59,490 --> 00:23:02,786
Below the funnel, messages are broken down by
delivery

341
00:23:02,816 --> 00:23:04,880
state, by channel and by language.

342
00:23:05,210 --> 00:23:09,1000
SMS, WhatsApp and phone calls carry the messages,
and they go out in English,

343
00:23:10,030 --> 00:23:13,380
Hindi and Arabic, each in the resident's own
language.

344
00:23:14,267 --> 00:23:16,972
Resident responses show how people answered:

345
00:23:17,002 --> 00:23:19,707
accept, decline, a question, or no response.

346
00:23:20,037 --> 00:23:24,331
Appointments are tracked by status, through to
completed, no show and cancelled,

347
00:23:24,361 --> 00:23:28,167
and the table below lists each appointment with its
clinic and service.

348
00:23:29,064 --> 00:23:32,851
To see what a resident actually receives, open the
care journey

349
00:23:32,881 --> 00:23:35,214
of a resident whose language is Arabic.

350
00:23:35,544 --> 00:23:39,317
The Act stage shows the approved reminder in three
languages,

351
00:23:39,347 --> 00:23:42,684
side by side, with the resident's own language
marked.

352
00:23:43,583 --> 00:23:48,013
English and Hindi read left to right, and Arabic
reads right to left.

353
00:23:48,343 --> 00:23:53,531
Every message starts with a simulated notice, and
each one is built from an approved

354
00:23:53,561 --> 00:23:56,823
template, so the wording is always the approved
text.

355
00:23:57,730 --> 00:24:01,780
The preview confirms that nothing is stored, sent or
drafted by

356
00:24:01,810 --> 00:24:04,630
an AI model, and that no AI calls were made.

357
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:08,783
Below it, the delivery timeline follows the message
from created,

358
00:24:08,813 --> 00:24:11,570
to sent, to delivered, to the resident's reply.

359
00:24:17,514 --> 00:24:20,084
Programs and impact brings the results together.

360
00:24:20,414 --> 00:24:24,077
The Impact tab lists every programme with its
eligible residents,

361
00:24:24,107 --> 00:24:27,032
follows the care journey from eligible to completed,

362
00:24:27,062 --> 00:24:30,554
and ranks districts, with equity and cohort
performance below.

363
00:24:30,884 --> 00:24:32,984
Each stage opens the cases behind it.

364
00:24:38,113 --> 00:24:41,113
Impact and economics is deliberately strict.

365
00:24:41,443 --> 00:24:46,885
Every figure was measured on the thirteenth of
November, each one shows what it was counted

366
00:24:46,915 --> 00:24:50,373
from, and the page states that nothing in it is
projected.

367
00:24:51,282 --> 00:24:54,602
Reading notes finds care gaps that records alone
miss.

368
00:24:54,932 --> 00:24:58,692
The same approved rules ran once on structured
records only,

369
00:24:58,722 --> 00:25:00,902
and once on records plus the notes.

370
00:25:01,232 --> 00:25:05,482
Records alone flag eighty one percent of the
residents who should be flagged.

371
00:25:05,812 --> 00:25:08,112
With the notes it is ninety nine percent.

372
00:25:09,011 --> 00:25:12,601
The notes also protect residents from unneeded
invitations.

373
00:25:12,931 --> 00:25:16,011
Records alone invite two hundred and sixty nine
residents

374
00:25:16,041 --> 00:25:17,811
who should not have been invited.

375
00:25:18,141 --> 00:25:19,421
With the notes, it is three.

376
00:25:19,751 --> 00:25:23,266
The trade is caution: screening decisions that are
undated

377
00:25:23,296 --> 00:25:25,161
or unclear are held for review.

378
00:25:26,048 --> 00:25:29,718
These gains come from family history that structured
records never hold.

379
00:25:30,048 --> 00:25:34,055
The notes surface genetic counselling referrals,
BRCA risk assessments

380
00:25:34,085 --> 00:25:37,400
and colorectal screening for people with a family
history,

381
00:25:37,430 --> 00:25:39,938
and records alone found none of those cases.

382
00:25:40,849 --> 00:25:45,758
The second panel sets the optimised plan against a
first come first served

383
00:25:45,788 --> 00:25:48,829
plan on the same care gaps and the same seats.

384
00:25:49,159 --> 00:25:55,437
The optimised plan places more care gaps, and it
cuts the district access gap from about thirteen

385
00:25:55,467 --> 00:25:59,469
points to under one point, with no rule breaks in
either plan.

386
00:26:00,350 --> 00:26:01,670
Cost is measured too.

387
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:05,603
Reading the notes costs under a third of a cent per
resident,

388
00:26:05,633 --> 00:26:10,010
across ten thousand residents and more than thirty
two thousand documents.

389
00:26:10,917 --> 00:26:14,337
Measured quality shows the evaluation run against
known answers.

390
00:26:14,667 --> 00:26:17,397
It checks the software and is not clinical
validation.

391
00:26:17,727 --> 00:26:22,341
This run used held out test answers on the synthetic
dataset, and it reports quote

392
00:26:22,371 --> 00:26:25,965
verification, subject accuracy, negation and
certainty accuracy,

393
00:26:25,995 --> 00:26:29,477
and the hallucination rate, each with its confidence
interval.

394
00:26:34,058 --> 00:26:36,958
Reports builds a shareable report from the live
data.

395
00:26:37,288 --> 00:26:41,165
Choose a report type, a programme, a geography and a
time period,

396
00:26:41,195 --> 00:26:43,208
then pick the sections to include.

397
00:26:43,538 --> 00:26:47,088
Tabs keep your saved reports and recent reports
close by.

398
00:26:47,976 --> 00:26:52,373
Report type covers population summary, programme
performance, district comparison,

399
00:26:52,403 --> 00:26:55,666
care gap analysis, equity and access, and monthly
operations.

400
00:26:55,996 --> 00:26:58,806
The report focus checkboxes decide which sections
appear.

401
00:27:00,953 --> 00:27:04,403
Generate report builds the preview from the same
live figures.

402
00:27:04,733 --> 00:27:08,327
The executive summary states the findings in plain
sentences,

403
00:27:08,357 --> 00:27:11,952
the population overview breaks residents down by age
and sex,

404
00:27:11,982 --> 00:27:16,053
and the care journey table gives each stage with its
count and share.

405
00:27:16,949 --> 00:27:19,199
Save report keeps it in Saved reports.

406
00:27:19,529 --> 00:27:23,340
Copy link gives a shareable address, Print produces
a clean page,

407
00:27:23,370 --> 00:27:26,589
and Download CSV exports the figures for a
spreadsheet.

408
00:27:27,491 --> 00:27:31,238
Quick reports at the bottom create the common ones
in a single click:

409
00:27:31,268 --> 00:27:33,537
population summary, programme performance,

410
00:27:33,567 --> 00:27:36,931
district comparison, care gap analysis and monthly
operations.

411
00:27:41,215 --> 00:27:44,848
Ask Anticipa is a question box for the population
data,

412
00:27:44,878 --> 00:27:48,045
and the robot button is available on every page.

413
00:27:48,375 --> 00:27:54,408
It answers from verified totals only, never from
individual records, and it does not give medical

414
00:27:54,438 --> 00:27:58,345
advice or say whether a particular person is due for
screening.

415
00:27:59,231 --> 00:28:01,331
Suggested questions help you start.

416
00:28:01,661 --> 00:28:03,949
I will ask about cervical cancer screening:

417
00:28:03,979 --> 00:28:06,751
what are its closure rate and its planning coverage?

418
00:28:11,750 --> 00:28:14,450
The answer states the figures in plain words.

419
00:28:14,780 --> 00:28:18,962
Under it, How I got this opens the exact query
behind the answer,

420
00:28:18,992 --> 00:28:22,460
and the answer reports that the figures are
simulated.

421
00:28:22,790 --> 00:28:25,350
Every number can be checked against its source.

422
00:28:26,250 --> 00:28:30,125
A second question: how full is clinic capacity in
Bengaluru

423
00:28:30,155 --> 00:28:32,110
Urban over the next two weeks?

424
00:28:32,440 --> 00:28:36,350
The answer comes from the capacity figures and names
the district.

425
00:28:39,574 --> 00:28:45,004
In an evaluation of twenty five questions, every
answer was correct and grounded in the

426
00:28:45,034 --> 00:28:49,084
data, and the five questions that should be refused
were refused.

427
00:28:49,414 --> 00:28:52,784
The cost averaged a small fraction of a cent per
question.

428
00:28:57,112 --> 00:29:01,842
Below the main pages sits the System section, which
only system administrators see.

429
00:29:02,172 --> 00:29:06,766
Live monitor shows the resident pipeline as it is
right now: residents loaded,

430
00:29:06,796 --> 00:29:10,322
batches, progress and the storage profile of what is
loaded.

431
00:29:10,652 --> 00:29:13,592
The figures refresh by themselves every ten seconds.

432
00:29:14,502 --> 00:29:18,714
The monitor also follows background jobs, AI spend
against the approved

433
00:29:18,744 --> 00:29:21,582
budget, and how many notes have extracted facts.

434
00:29:22,485 --> 00:29:27,759
Jobs lists every background task: ingestion,
evidence extraction, rule evaluation and

435
00:29:27,789 --> 00:29:31,565
the engagement simulation, each with its status and
progress.

436
00:29:31,895 --> 00:29:33,475
A failed job is never hidden.

437
00:29:33,805 --> 00:29:36,165
It also raises an alert on the Command Center.

438
00:29:36,495 --> 00:29:38,335
Open a job to follow its timeline.

439
00:29:40,133 --> 00:29:43,066
Audit is a permanent record of sensitive actions:

440
00:29:43,096 --> 00:29:46,393
who did what, to which resource, and with what
outcome.

441
00:29:46,723 --> 00:29:51,053
Entries cannot be edited or deleted, and each one
links to its request.

442
00:29:53,240 --> 00:29:59,214
Integrations checks the services Anticipa depends
on: the PostgreSQL database with vector search,

443
00:29:59,244 --> 00:30:03,360
the HAPI F H I R server, Anthropic Claude and Nebius
Token Factory.

444
00:30:03,690 --> 00:30:07,160
The checks are lightweight connectivity tests and
never use model quota.

445
00:30:12,279 --> 00:30:13,419
That is the whole loop.

446
00:30:13,749 --> 00:30:17,823
Anticipa reads records and clinical notes and keeps
every extracted

447
00:30:17,853 --> 00:30:20,029
fact tied to the quote it came from.

448
00:30:20,359 --> 00:30:22,960
Approved, versioned rules decide who is due,

449
00:30:22,990 --> 00:30:26,309
and every decision names its guideline and rule
version.

450
00:30:27,198 --> 00:30:30,554
The planner places cases within real clinic capacity
and

451
00:30:30,584 --> 00:30:32,548
reports who was left out and why.

452
00:30:32,878 --> 00:30:38,009
Outreach reaches residents in their own language,
hands medical questions to a clinician,

453
00:30:38,039 --> 00:30:41,488
and tracks every case until completion evidence is
verified.

454
00:30:42,390 --> 00:30:45,933
Everything is auditable, every role sees only what
it needs,

455
00:30:45,963 --> 00:30:49,210
and every number here can be traced back to its
source.

456
00:30:49,540 --> 00:30:52,490
Read, decide, plan, act, and close the loop.

457
00:30:52,820 --> 00:30:54,140
Thank you for watching.
